{
  "requirement": "`habeas status` and the mdBook requirements index MUST generate their Definition/Evidence Markdown tables through the same renderer. That renderer MUST calculate column widths, align the columns, and display the same evidence-state labels in both outputs. Callers MAY select different definitions and supply different kind-labeled name cells so mdBook can link to detail pages.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is met. The one weakness is test tagging, covered at the end.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `src/markdown.rs` defines a single `render_evidence_table(rows: &[(String, &str)])`.\n  - It computes each column width as the widest cell, with the header names as the minimum.\n  - It left-pads every cell and emits the header, a `---` separator row and the data rows with identical widths.\n- `habeas status` calls it from `status_markdown_with_options` (`src/commands.rs`, around line 96).\n  - Its name cell is `` `name` (kind) ``.\n  - Its state cell is `evidence.state().label()`.\n- The mdBook index calls it from `IndexPlan::populate` (`src/index.rs`, around line 352).\n  - Its name cell is a link of the form ``[`name`](href) (kind)``.\n  - Its state cell is also `evidence.state().label()`.\n- Both callers import the same function. Both take labels from the same `EvidenceState::label()`, so the labels cannot differ.\n- Callers choose their own definitions and supply their own name cells. `status` filters by `--all`, glob and verbose. The index lists every detail-page definition and links each one. This is the flexibility the requirement allows.\n\n**Tests**\n- `index_shows_evidence_states_and_links_generated_chapters` in `src/index.rs` is tagged `{test}` for this requirement.\n  - It checks each row's label in the index: Valid, Determination false, Invalid and Missing.\n  - It checks that every table line has its `|` delimiters at identical positions.\n  - It checks that the name cells are linked.\n- `tests/commands.rs` tests the CLI side.\n  - `status_lists_nonvalid_requirements_and_all_option_in_name_order` checks the same label vocabulary in `status` output.\n  - `status_tables_align_each_column_to_its_widest_cell` checks alignment, widest-cell widths and the `---` separator for both `status` and `status --all`.\n  - These two are tagged for `requirements.cli.status` and `requirements.cli.status_table_alignment`, not for this requirement. They still exercise the CLI path through the shared renderer.\n\nBoth outputs are exercised against the same alignment and label expectations, and the shared function makes the \"same renderer\" property structural.\n\n**Weakness:** no single test compares the two outputs directly. I judge that unnecessary, because the shared call and shared `label()` already guarantee it.",
